"# Slice Notation in Python\n\nPython's slice notation allows you to extract a subset of elements from a list or string.\n\n### Basic Slice Notation\n\n```python\na = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nprint(a[1:3])  # Output: [2, 3]\n```\n\nIn the above example, `a[1:3]` means start at index 1 and go up to, but not including, index 3.\n\n### Omitting Start Index\n\n```python\na = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nprint(a[1:])  # Output: [2, 3, 4, 5]\n```\n\nWhen you omit the start index, it defaults to the beginning of the list.\n\n### Omitting End Index\n\n```python\na = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nprint(a[:3])  # Output: [1, 2, 3]\n```\n\nWhen you omit the end index, it defaults to the end of the list.\n\n### Step Parameter\n\n```python\na = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nprint(a[::2])  # Output: [1, 3, 5]\n```\n\nThe step parameter specifies the increment between elements. If omitted, it defaults to 1.\n\n### Negative Indices\n\n```python\na = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nprint(a[-2:])  # Output: [4, 5]\n```\n\nNegative indices count from the end of the list.\n\n### Reassigning Slice Notation\n\n```python\na = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]\na[1:2] = [['b', 'c']]\nprint(a)  # Output: [[1, 2], ['b', 'c'], [5, 6]]\n```\n\nReassigning slice notation can modify the original list."
