"from django.http import HttpResponse\nfrom django.shortcuts import render\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.core.files.storage import default_storage\nfrom django.core.files.uploadedfile import UploadedFile\nimport os\n\ndef index(request):\n    if request.method == 'POST':\n        file = request.FILES.get('file')\n        if file:\n            # Save the file to the server\n            default_storage.save(os.path.basename(file.name), file)\n            return HttpResponse(\"File uploaded successfully\")\n    return render(request, 'index.html')"
"```python\nfrom django.db import models\n\nclass Car(models.Model):\n    manufacturer = models.ForeignKey(\n        'Manufacturer',\n        on_delete=models.CASCADE,\n    )\n    # ...\n\nclass Manufacturer(models.Model):\n    # ...\n    pass\n```\n\nThe `on_delete` parameter in Django models determines what action to take when the referenced object is deleted.\n\nIn the provided example, `on_delete=models.CASCADE` means that if a `Manufacturer` instance is deleted, all `Car` instances associated with it will also be deleted. This is equivalent to the behavior of `models.PROTECT` (protect the referenced object from being deleted) and `models.SET_NULL` (set the foreign key to `NULL`).\n\nOther available options for `on_delete` include:\n\n- `models.PROTECT`: Protect the referenced object from being deleted.\n- `models.SET_NULL`: Set the foreign key to `NULL`.\n- `models.SET_DEFAULT`: Set the foreign key to its default value.\n- `models.DELETE_NULL`: Delete the foreign key if the referenced object is deleted.\n- `models.DO_NOTHING`: Do nothing when the referenced object is deleted.\n\nThe Django documentation for this can be found at: https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/ref/models/fields/#django.db.models.ForeignKey"
