"# Debugging in Django: A Better Approach\n\n## Step 1: Enable Debug Mode\n\n```python\nimport os\n\n# Enable debug mode\nif 'DEBUG' not in os.environ:\n    os.environ['DEBUG'] = 'True'\n```\n\n## Step 2: Use a Debugger\n\nDjango comes with a built-in debugger that can help you identify issues in your code. You can use it by adding the following code to your `settings.py` file:\n\n```python\nDEBUG = True\n\n# Run the following command in your terminal\n# python manage.py runserver\n```\n\n## Step 3: Use a Code Editor with Debugging Capabilities\n\nMany code editors, such as PyCharm, Visual Studio Code, and Sublime Text, have built-in debugging capabilities. These can help you step through your code, set breakpoints, and inspect variables.\n\n## Step 4: Use a Logging Library\n\nDjango's logging library can help you identify issues in your code. You can use it by adding the following code to your `settings.py` file:\n\n```python\nLOGGING = {\n    'version': 1,\n    'disable_existing_loggers': False,\n    'handlers': {\n        'console': {\n            'class': 'logging.StreamHandler',\n        },\n    },\n    'loggers': {\n        'django': {\n            'handlers': ['console'],\n            'level': 'INFO',\n        },\n    },\n}\n```\n\n## Step 5: Use a Testing Framework\n\nWriting unit tests and integration tests can help you identify issues in your code before they cause problems in production.\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\nclass MyModelTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_my_model(self):\n        # Test code here\n        pass\n```\n\n## Step 6: Use a Version Control System\n\nUsing a version control system, such as Git, can help you track changes to your code and identify issues that may have arisen due to changes.\n\n```bash\n# Initialize a new Git repository\ngit init\n\n# Add all files to the repository\ngit add .\n\n# Commit changes\ngit commit -m \"Initial commit\"\n```"
"```python\nimport subprocess\nimport os\n\n# Define the paths to your virtual environment and requirements file\nvirtualenv_path = '/path/to/your/virtualenv'\nrequirements_path = '/path/to/your/requirements/file'\n\n# Run the pip command with the --upgrade flag\ncommand = f'pip --install --upgrade -E {virtualenv_path} --requirement {requirements_path}'\n\n# Use subprocess to run the command and capture the output\noutput = subprocess.check_output(command, shell=True)\n\n# Print the output to the console\nprint(output.decode('utf-8'))\n```\n\nThis code uses the subprocess module to run the pip command with the --upgrade flag, and captures the output. This will print the output of the command to the console, which includes any source code conflicts. \n\nHowever, this approach may not be suitable for production use, as it prints the output to the console. A better approach would be to redirect the output to a file, and then parse the output to determine if there were any source code conflicts.\n\nAlternatively, you could use the following command to upgrade all packages in the requirements file, including Django:\n\n```bash\npip --upgrade -r requirements.txt\n```\n\nThis command will upgrade all packages listed in the requirements file, including Django, and will not attempt to downgrade any packages that are already newer than the ones listed in the requirements file."
"```python\nfrom django.contrib.auth import update_session_auth_hash\nfrom django.contrib.auth.forms import PasswordResetForm\nfrom django.contrib.auth.tokens import default_token_generator\nfrom django.contrib.auth.views import PasswordResetConfirmView, PasswordResetConfirmSuccessView\nfrom django.contrib.sites.shortcuts import get_current_site\nfrom django.core.mail import EmailMessage\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse\nfrom django.shortcuts import render, redirect\nfrom django.template.loader import render_to_string\nfrom django.utils.encoding import force_bytes\nfrom django.utils.http import urlsafe_base64_encode\nfrom django.utils.safestring import mark_safe\n\ndef reset_password(request):\n    if request.method == 'POST':\n        password_reset_form = PasswordResetForm(request.POST)\n        if password_reset_form.is_valid():\n            data = password_reset_form.cleaned_data['email']\n            associated_users = User.objects.filter(email=data)\n            if associated_users.exists():\n                for user in associated_users:\n                    subject = 'Password Reset Requested'\n                    email_template_name = 'password_reset_email.txt'\n                    c = {\n                        'email': user.email,\n                        'domain': get_current_site(request).domain,\n                        'site_name': 'Your Site Name',\n                        'uid': urlsafe_base64_encode(force_bytes(user.pk)),\n                        'token': default_token_generator.make_token(user),\n                    }\n                    email = render_to_string(email_template_name, c)\n                    try:\n                        send_email(subject, email, 'from@example.com', [user.email])\n                    except Exception as e:\n                        print(e)\n                    return redirect('/password_reset/done/')\n    else:\n        password_reset_form = PasswordResetForm()\n    return render(request, 'password_reset.html', {'password_reset_form': password_reset_form})\n\nclass MyPasswordResetConfirmView(PasswordResetConfirmView):\n    template_name = 'password_reset_confirm.html'\n\nclass MyPasswordResetConfirmSuccessView(PasswordResetConfirmSuccessView):\n    template_name = 'password_reset_done.html'\n\ndef make_admin(user):\n    user.is_staff = True\n    user.save()\n\ndef remove_admin(user):\n    user.is_staff = False\n    user.save()\n```\n\nExplanation:\nTo reset the admin password in Django, you need to create a password reset form and send a password reset email to the admin's email address. Then, the admin can reset the password by clicking on the link provided in the email.\n\nTo make a normal user into an admin, you can call the `make_admin` function and pass the user object as an argument.\n\nTo remove the admin status from a user, you can call the `remove_admin` function and pass the user object as an argument.\n\nPlease note that you need to replace 'Your Site Name' with your actual site name in the email template.\n\nAlso, make sure to create the necessary email templates (e.g., `password_reset_email.txt`, `password_reset_done.html`, `password_reset_confirm.html`) in your templates directory.\n\nYou may need to adjust the code according to your project's structure and requirements."
