"import pandas as pd\nfrom tabulate import tabulate\nfrom prettytable import PrettyTable\n\ndef pretty_print_series(series):\n    # Create a PrettyTable instance\n    table = PrettyTable()\n    # Add column headers\n    table.field_names = series.columns.tolist()\n    # Add data rows\n    for index, row in series.items():\n        table.add_row(row)\n    # Print the table\n    print(table)\n\ndef pretty_print_dataframe(df):\n    # Create a PrettyTable instance\n    table = PrettyTable()\n    # Add column headers\n    table.field_names = df.columns.tolist()\n    # Add data rows\n    for index, row in df.iterrows():\n        table.add_row(row)\n    # Print the table\n    print(table)\n\ndef pretty_print_with_color(df):\n    # Create a PrettyTable instance\n    table = PrettyTable()\n    # Add column headers\n    table.field_names = df.columns.tolist()\n    # Add data rows with color-coding\n    for index, row in df.iterrows():\n        colors = [\"green\" if x == df.iloc[0, 0] else \"red\" for x in row]\n        table.add_row([f\"\\033[92m{x}\\033[0m\" if c == \"green\" else f\"\\033[91m{x}\\033[0m\" for x, c in zip(row, colors)])\n    # Print the table\n    print(table)\n\n# Example usage\nseries = pd.Series([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], index=[0, 1, 2, 3, 4])\npretty_print_series(series)\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"A\": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],\n    \"B\": [6, 7, 8, 9, 10],\n    \"C\": [11, 12, 13, 14, 15]\n})\npretty_print_dataframe(df)\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"A\": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],\n    \"B\": [6, 7, 8, 9, 10],\n    \"C\": [11, 12, 13, 14, 15]\n})\npretty_print_with_color(df)"
"# Large Data Workflows using pandas\n\n## Loading Flat Files into a Permanent, On-Disk Database Structure\n\nWe can use `pytables` to create a permanent, on-disk database structure. Here's an example of how to load a flat file and store it in a `pytables` table:\n\n```python\nimport pytables as pta\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Load the flat file\ndf = pd.read_csv('data.csv', chunksize=10000)\n\n# Create a pytables table\nwith pta.HDFStore('data.h5', 'w') as store:\n    for i, chunk in enumerate(df):\n        table = store.create_table('table', columns=df.columns)\n        table[i] = chunk\n```\n\n## Querying the Database to Retrieve Data to Feed into a pandas DataFrame\n\nWe can use the `pytables` API to query the database and retrieve data to feed into a pandas DataFrame. Here's an example of how to do this:\n\n```python\nimport pytables as pta\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Load the data from the pytables table\nwith pta.HDFStore('data.h5') as store:\n    table = store['table']\n    data = table.read()\n\n# Convert the data to a pandas DataFrame\ndf = pd.DataFrame(data)\n```\n\n## Updating the Database after Manipulating Pieces in pandas\n\nWe can use the `pytables` API to update the database after manipulating pieces in pandas. Here's an example of how to do this:\n\n```python\nimport pytables as pta\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Load the data from the pytables table\nwith pta.HDFStore('data.h5') as store:\n    table = store['table']\n\n# Manipulate the data in pandas\ndf = pd.read_csv('data.csv', chunksize=10000)\nfor i, chunk in enumerate(df):\n    new_column = chunk['column1'] + chunk['column2']\n    chunk['new_column'] = new_column\n    table[i] = chunk\n\n# Update the database\nstore.flush()\n```\n\n## Iteratively Importing a Large Flat File and Storing it in a Permanent, On-Disk Database Structure\n\nWe can use a loop to iteratively import a large flat file and store it in a permanent, on-disk database structure:\n\n```python\nimport pytables as pta\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Load the flat file and store it in a pytables table\nwith pta.HDFStore('data.h5', 'w') as store:\n    chunksize = 10000\n    for i in range(0, 100000, chunksize):\n        df = pd.read_csv('data.csv', chunksize=chunksize)\n        table = store.create_table('table', columns=df.columns)\n        for j, chunk in enumerate(df):\n            table[j + i] = chunk\n```\n\n## Creating New Columns by Performing Various Operations on Selected Columns\n\nWe can use pandas to create new columns by performing various operations on selected columns:\n\n```python\nimport pytables as pta\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Load the data from the pytables table\nwith pta.HDFStore('data.h5') as store:\n    table = store['table']\n\n# Create new columns\ndf = pd.read_csv('data.csv', chunksize=10000)\nfor i, chunk in enumerate(df):\n    new_column = chunk['column1'] + chunk['column2']\n    chunk['new_column'] = new_column\n    table[i] = chunk\n```\n\n## Appending New Columns into the Database Structure\n\nWe can use the `pytables` API to append new columns into the database structure. Here's an example of how to do this:\n\n```python\nimport pytables as pta\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Load the data from the pytables table\nwith pta.HDFStore('data.h5') as store:\n    table = store['table']\n\n# Create new columns\ndf = pd.read_csv('data.csv', chunksize=10000)\nfor i, chunk in enumerate(df):\n    new_column = chunk['column1'] + chunk['column2']\n    chunk['new_column'] = new_column\n    table.append(chunk)\n```\n\n## Creating Candidate Variables that Explain the Relationship Between Data and Outcome\n\nWe can use pandas to create candidate variables that explain the relationship between data and outcome. Here's an example of how to do this:\n\n```python\nimport pytables as pta\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Load the data from the pytables table\nwith pta.HDFStore('data.h5') as store:\n    table = store['table']\n\n# Create candidate variables\ndf = pd.read_csv('data.csv', chunksize=10000)\nfor i, chunk in enumerate(df):\n    new_column = chunk['column1'] + chunk['column2']\n    chunk['new_column'] = new_column\n    table[i] = chunk\n\n# Apply learning techniques to create an equation out of the candidate variables\nequation = pd.DataFrame({'equation': [new_column]})\nstore.append(equation)\n```"
