"# C++11 Memory Model Overview\n\n## What is the C++11 Memory Model?\n\nThe C++11 memory model is a standardized library that provides a way to control how different threads interact with the processor's memory. This standardization ensures that C++ code can be compiled and run on any platform, regardless of who made the compiler and on what type of system it's running.\n\n## Benefits of the C++11 Memory Model\n\nThe C++11 memory model provides several benefits, including:\n\n*   Standardization: Ensures that C++ code can be compiled and run on any platform, without modification.\n*   Optimization: Allows for optimization of code without breaking assumptions made by the programmer.\n*   Thread Safety: Provides a way to ensure that threads interact with memory safely and correctly.\n\n## Low-Level Details\n\nThe C++11 memory model is based on the following assumptions:\n\n*   **Sequence Points**: The order in which statements are executed is defined.\n*   **Memory Access**: The order in which memory is accessed is defined.\n*   **Thread Safety**: The way in which threads interact with memory is defined.\n\n## Relation to Multi-Threading Support\n\nThe C++11 memory model is closely related to the C++11 multi-threading support. The memory model provides a way to ensure that threads interact with memory safely and correctly, which is essential for multi-threaded programming.\n\n## C++11 Thread Model Assumptions\n\nThe C++11 thread model is based on the following assumptions:\n\n*   **Thread Local Storage**: Each thread has its own storage space.\n*   **Shared Variables**: Variables shared by multiple threads.\n*   **Synchronization**: Mechanisms for synchronizing access to shared variables.\n\n## Example Code\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <thread>\n\n// Shared variable\nint shared_var = 0;\n\n// Function to increment shared variable\nvoid increment_shared_var() {\n    for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {\n        shared_var++;\n    }\n}\n\nint main() {\n    // Create threads\n    std::thread thread1(increment_shared_var);\n    std::thread thread2(increment_shared_var);\n\n    // Wait for threads to finish\n    thread1.join();\n    thread2.join();\n\n    // Print final value of shared variable\n    std::cout << \"Final value of shared variable: \" << shared_var << std::endl;\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, we have two threads that increment a shared variable. The C++11 memory model ensures that the final value of the shared variable is correct, even in the presence of concurrent access."
