"from typing import TypeVar, Generic\n\nT = TypeVar('T')\n\nclass CustomList(Generic[T]):\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.items = []\n\n    def append(self, item: T) -> None:\n        self.items.append(item)\n\n    def extend(self, items: Iterable[T]) -> None:\n        self.items.extend(items)\n\n    def __len__(self) -> int:\n        return len(self.items)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, index: int) -> T:\n        return self.items[index]\n\n    def __setitem__(self, index: int, value: T) -> None:\n        self.items[index] = value\n\n    def __delitem__(self, index: int) -> None:\n        del self.items[index]\n\n    def insert(self, index: int, value: T) -> None:\n        self.items.insert(index, value)\n\n    def remove(self, value: T) -> bool:\n        return value in self.items and self.items.remove(value)\n\n    def pop(self, index: int = -1) -> T:\n        return self.items.pop(index)\n\n    def index(self, value: T) -> int:\n        return self.items.index(value)\n\n    def count(self, value: T) -> int:\n        return self.items.count(value)\n\n    def __contains__(self, value: T) -> bool:\n        return value in self.items\n\n    def __iter__(self) -> iter[T]:\n        return iter(self.items)\n\n    def __reversed__(self) -> reversed[T]:\n        return reversed(self.items)\n\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\n        return repr(self.items)\n\n    def __str__(self) -> str:\n        return str(self.items)assistant\n\nThe guideline against inheriting from `List<T>` is primarily due to performance and maintainability concerns.\n\nWhen you inherit from `List<T>`, you're essentially adding your own properties and methods on top of the existing list implementation. However, this can lead to several issues:\n\n1.  **Performance overhead**: By adding your own properties and methods, you're introducing additional overhead in terms of memory management, copying, and other operations. This can lead to performance degradation, especially when dealing with large datasets.\n2.  **Inconsistent behavior**: If you add custom behavior that modifies the underlying list, you risk breaking the expected behavior of the `List<T>` class. For example, if you override the `Remove` method, you might inadvertently remove items from the list that shouldn't be removed.\n3.  **Maintenance and debugging difficulties**: When you inherit from `List<T>`, you're tightly coupled to the existing implementation. If Microsoft changes the `List<T>` class in the future, your custom class might break or behave unexpectedly.\n\nInstead of inheriting from `List<T>`, consider using the `Collection<T>` class, which provides a more flexible and customizable implementation for custom collections. However, as you mentioned, `Collection<T>` is a bare-bones class that lacks some useful features, such as `AddRange`.\n\nImplementing `IList<T>` can also be an option, but as you pointed out, it requires a significant amount of boilerplate code.\n\nWrapping the `List<T>` in a custom class, like you suggested, can be a viable approach. This approach allows you to add custom properties and methods without tightly coupling your class to the underlying list implementation. However, as you noted, it can lead to verbosity and may not be the most intuitive way to think about the world.\n\nUltimately, the decision to inherit from `List<T>` depends on your specific requirements and constraints. If you need to add custom properties and methods that don't modify the underlying list, you might be able to get away with inheriting from `List<T>`. However, if you anticipate changing the list implementation or need to add custom behavior that modifies the list, it's generally recommended to use a more flexible and customizable approach."
