"```python\n# Note: This is a Python code for getting all form inputs using the requests library.\nimport requests\nimport json\n\ndef get_form_inputs():\n    url = \"https://example.com/myurl\"  # replace with your URL\n    form_data = {}\n    \n    # Get all form inputs\n    form_inputs = requestAnimationFrame(lambda: requestAnimationFrame(None))\n    while True:\n        try:\n            inputs = requestAnimationFrame(lambda: requestAnimationFrame(None)).elements\n        except:\n            break\n        for input in inputs:\n            if input.name in form_data:\n                form_data[input.name].append(input.value)\n            else:\n                form_data[input.name] = [input.value]\n    \n    return form_data\n\ndef send_form_data(form_data):\n    # Assuming you have a URL to send the data to\n    url = \"https://example.com/submit\"\n    response = requests.post(url, data=form_data)\n    return response.text\n\n# Usage\nform_data = get_form_inputs()\nresponse = send_form_data(form_data)\nprint(response)\n```\n\nNote: The above code is a basic example. In a real-world scenario, you would need to handle errors and exceptions, and also ensure that the data is being sent correctly.\n\nHowever, if you want to achieve this in a more traditional jQuery AJAX manner, you can use the following code:\n\n```javascript\n$(document).ready(function(){\n    var form_data = {};\n    $(\"#orderproductForm input\").each(function() {\n        form_data[this.name] = this.value;\n    });\n    \n    $.ajax({\n        type: \"POST\",\n        url: \"myurl\",\n        data: form_data,\n        success: function(data) {\n            console.log(data);\n        }\n    });\n});\n```"
