"```python\nimport random\nimport time\n\n# Function to generate a matrix of O and #\ndef generate_matrix(size):\n    matrix = []\n    for _ in range(size):\n        row = []\n        for _ in range(size):\n            if random.randint(0, 3) == 0:\n                row.append('O')\n            else:\n                row.append('#')\n        matrix.append(''.join(row))\n    return '\\n'.join(matrix)\n\n# Function to generate a matrix of O and B\ndef generate_matrix_b(size):\n    matrix = []\n    for _ in range(size):\n        row = []\n        for _ in range(size):\n            if random.randint(0, 3) == 0:\n                row.append('O')\n            else:\n                row.append('B')\n        matrix.append(''.join(row))\n    return '\\n'.join(matrix)\n\n# Test the functions\nsize = 1000\nmatrix_o = generate_matrix(size)\nmatrix_b = generate_matrix_b(size)\n\n# Measure the time taken to print the matrices\nstart_time = time.time()\nprint(matrix_o)\nprint(matrix_b)\nend_time = time.time()\n\nprint(f\"Time taken to print matrix O: {end_time - start_time} seconds\")\nprint(f\"Time taken to print matrix B: {end_time - start_time} seconds\")\n```\n\nThe reason behind the dramatically different run times is due to the way Java handles Unicode characters. The character 'B' is a Unicode character that requires more processing power to print than the character '#'. In Java, when you print a character using `System.out.print()`, it first checks if the character is a Unicode character. If it is, it then calls the `unicodeCharAt()` method to get the Unicode code point of the character, which can be a time-consuming operation. This is why printing 'B' is slower than printing '#'."
