"# HTTP GET with Request Body\n\n## Is this a good idea altogether?\n\nWhile HTTP/1.1 does not explicitly forbid using request bodies in GET requests, it is generally considered a bad practice. The primary reason is that GET requests are meant to retrieve information and should not modify the server in any way.\n\n## Will HTTP clients have issues with using request bodies within a GET request?\n\nHTTP clients may not have issues, but it's not a standard behavior and might not be supported by all clients or servers. The main issue is that the request body can be used to send data that could potentially be used for malicious purposes, such as sending a malicious payload or bypassing security restrictions.\n\n```python\nimport http.client\nimport urllib.parse\n\ndef send_get_request(url, params=None, data=None):\n    if params is not None:\n        # Parse query string parameters\n        parsed_url = urllib.parse.urlparse(url)\n        query_params = urllib.parse.parse_qs(parsed_url.query)\n        query_params.update(params)\n        url += urllib.parse.urlencode(query_params, doseq=True)\n    else:\n        # No query string parameters\n\n    if data is not None:\n        # Send data in the request body\n        headers = {'Content-Type': 'application/json'}\n        conn = http.client.HTTPSConnection(url)\n        conn.request('GET', url, json=data, headers=headers)\n        response = conn.getresponse()\n        return response.read()\n    else:\n        # No request body\n\n    conn.close()\n\n# Example usage\nurl = 'https://example.com'\nparams = {'sort': 'name', 'limit': 10}\ndata = {'query': 'search_term'}\n\nresponse = send_get_request(url, params, data)\nprint(response)\n```"
