"# Definition and Usage of Faking, Mocking, and Stubbing in Unit Tests\n\n### Faking\n```python\nclass FakeUserRepository:\n    def get_user(self, user_id):\n        return {\"id\": user_id, \"name\": \"John Doe\", \"is_active\": True}\n\n    def update_user(self, user_id, updated_data):\n        return None\n```\n\nUse faking when you need to abstract out data dependencies and want to simplify your test setup. Faking is typically used when you have a data-driven test that doesn't require dynamic behavior.\n\n### Mocking\n```python\nimport unittest\nfrom unittest.mock import MagicMock\n\nclass UserService:\n    def __init__(self, user_repository):\n        self.user_repository = user_repository\n\n    def get_user(self, user_id):\n        return self.user_repository.get_user(user_id)\n\nclass MockUserRepository:\n    def get_user(self, user_id):\n        return {\"id\": user_id, \"name\": \"John Doe\", \"is_active\": True}\n\n    def update_user(self, user_id, updated_data):\n        return None\n\nclass TestUserService(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_get_user(self):\n        user_repository = MockUserRepository()\n        service = UserService(user_repository)\n        result = service.get_user(1)\n        self.assertEqual(result[\"id\"], 1)\n        self.assertEqual(result[\"name\"], \"John Doe\")\n        self.assertTrue(result[\"is_active\"])\n\n    def test_update_user(self):\n        user_repository = MockUserRepository()\n        user_repository.update_user.return_value = None\n        service = UserService(user_repository)\n        result = service.update_user(1, {\"name\": \"Jane Doe\"})\n        self.assertIsNone(result)\n```\n\nUse mocking when you need to verify interactions between your class and dependent classes. Mocking allows you to set up and verify the behavior of the dependent class.\n\n### Stubbing\n```python\nclass UserService:\n    def __init__(self, user_repository):\n        self.user_repository = user_repository\n\n    def get_user(self, user_id):\n        return self.user_repository.get_user(user_id)\n\nclass StubUserRepository:\n    def get_user(self, user_id):\n        return {\"id\": user_id, \"name\": \"John Doe\", \"is_active\": True}\n\nclass TestUserService(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_get_user(self):\n        user_repository = StubUserRepository()\n        service = UserService(user_repository)\n        result = service.get_user(1)\n        self.assertEqual(result[\"id\"], 1)\n        self.assertEqual(result[\"name\"], \"John Doe\")\n        self.assertTrue(result[\"is_active\"])\n```\n\nUse stubbing when you have verified the interactions with the dependent class and want to test alternate paths through your code. Stubbing is similar to mocking but does not provide the ability to verify method calls.\n\n### Generating Mocks and Stubs\nYou can generate mocks and stubs using a mocking framework such as `unittest.mock` in Python."
