"import random\n\ndef calculate_code_coverage():\n    # Assume a typical project with 1000 lines of code\n    total_lines = 1000\n    \n    # For simplicity, let's assume 80% of the code is covered by unit tests\n    # This is a reasonable estimate for many projects\n    code_coverage = 0.8\n    \n    # Calculate the minimum number of lines that need to be covered\n    min_covered_lines = int(code_coverage * total_lines)\n    \n    # Generate a random number of lines that need to be covered\n    min_covered_lines = random.randint(min_covered_lines, total_lines)\n    \n    return min_covered_lines\n\n# Example usage\nprint(\"Minimum code coverage:\", calculate_code_coverage())"
"# Mocking in Python\nMocking is a technique used to isolate dependencies in a system, allowing you to test and maintain your code more efficiently. It involves creating fake or mock objects that mimic the behavior of real objects, but without actually interacting with them.\n\n# What is Mocking?\nMocking is used to create a fake object that can be used in place of a real object, allowing you to isolate dependencies and test your code more efficiently.\n\n# Why is Mocking Useful?\nMocking is useful because it allows you to:\n- Isolate dependencies and make your code more modular\n- Test your code without relying on external dependencies\n- Reduce the impact of slow or unreliable dependencies on your tests\n\n# Example of Mocking in Python\n\n```python\nimport unittest\nfrom unittest.mock import Mock\n\ndef add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nclass TestAddFunction(unittest.TestCase):\n\n    def test_add(self):\n        mock_object = Mock()\n        mock_object.add.return_value = 10\n        self.assertEqual(add(mock_object, 5), 10)\n        mock_object.add.assert_called_once_with(5)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```"
