"```python\n# No Python code is required to run Gradle commands, but here's how you can use Gradle's test task to run only one unit test class.\n```\n\nTo run only one unit test class using Gradle, you can use the `test` task with the `includes` configuration property. Here's an example:\n\n```groovy\ntest {\n    includes '**/MyUnitClass.java'\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, replace `MyUnitClass.java` with the name of your unit test class.\n\nAlternatively, you can use the `test` task with the `filter` configuration property to run only specific test classes:\n\n```groovy\ntest {\n    filter = '/MyUnitClass.java'\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, replace `/MyUnitClass.java` with the fully qualified name of your unit test class.\n\nTo run these custom configurations, you can add them to your `build.gradle` file:\n\n```groovy\ntest {\n    includes '**/MyUnitClass.java'\n}\n```\n\nor\n\n```groovy\ntest {\n    filter = '/MyUnitClass.java'\n}\n```\n\nThen, run the test task using the following command:\n\n```bash\n./gradlew test\n```"
