"The term 'logits' in TensorFlow refers to the output of a linear layer in a neural network before applying an activation function. In other words, logits are the raw, unnormalized scores that represent the probability of an event occurring.\n\nIn the context of the softmax function used in the provided code, logits are not a mathematical function themselves but rather the input to the softmax function. The softmax function then normalizes these logits to produce a probability distribution over all classes.\n\nThink of logits as the \"raw\" or \"unnormalized\" scores that the model outputs, and the softmax function as the \"normalizer\" that scales these scores to produce a probability distribution."
"def max_pool_with_same_padding(input, kernel_size):\n    return tf.nn.max_pool(input, [1, kernel_size, kernel_size, 1], [1, kernel_size, kernel_size, 1], 'SAME')\n\ndef max_pool_with_valid_padding(input, kernel_size):\n    return tf.nn.max_pool(input, [1, kernel_size, kernel_size, 1], [1, kernel_size, kernel_size, 1], 'VALID')assistant\n\nThe difference between 'SAME' and 'VALID' padding in tf.nn.max_pool of tensorflow is as follows:\n\n- 'SAME' padding means that the output will have the same spatial dimensions as the input. This is achieved by adding padding to the input so that the spatial dimensions remain the same after max pooling. The number of padding is calculated as (kernel_size - 1) // 2.\n\n- 'VALID' padding, on the other hand, means that there will be no zero padding outside the edges when we do max pool. This is achieved by not adding any padding to the input. The output spatial dimensions will be (input spatial dimension - kernel_size + 1).\n\nIn summary, 'SAME' padding maintains the spatial dimensions of the input, while 'VALID' padding does not add any padding and thus reduces the spatial dimensions of the input."
