"import tensorflow as tf\n\ndef model(x):\n    x = tf.nn.batch_normalization(x, tf.reduce_mean(x, axis=[1, 2, 3], keepdims=True), \n                                 tf.reduce_mean(tf.square(x), axis=[1, 2, 3], keepdims=True), \n                                 0, 1, training=True)\n    x = tf.nn.dropout(x, 0.2)\n    return xassistant\n\nThe TensorFlow batch normalization layer does not automatically compensate for the potential issues described in the question. The batch normalization layer normalizes the input data along the specified axes (default is the last three axes), then scales the normalized data by a learned gamma and shifts it by a learned beta.\n\nThe order of batch normalization and dropout matters. If you use dropout followed by batch normalization, the dropout mask will be applied before the batch normalization, which can lead to biased results. This is because the dropout mask will only be applied to the data after it has been normalized, which can result in different values for the same input.\n\nTo avoid this issue, it's generally recommended to apply dropout before batch normalization. This ensures that the dropout mask is applied before any normalization or scaling is done, resulting in more accurate and unbiased results.\n\nUsing batch normalization and dropout on multiple successive layers does not necessarily pose any additional problems. However, it's worth noting that applying batch normalization after a dropout layer can lead to reduced effectiveness of the dropout, as the normalized data may not be representative of the original data due to the dropout mask.\n\nThe experimental test you mentioned suggests that ordering does matter, with validation loss increasing when dropout is before batch normalization. This is consistent with the recommended practice of applying dropout before batch normalization to avoid biased results."
