"```python\n# The super() function is used to call methods of parent classes in Python.\n# It allows us to avoid having to explicitly call the parent class's methods.\n\n# There is a difference between using Base.__init__ and super().__init__.\n# Using super().__init__ is considered better practice because it follows Python's method resolution order (MRO) and makes the code more flexible.\n\nclass Base(object):\n    def __init__(self):\n        print(\"Base created\")\n\nclass ChildA(Base):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n\nclass ChildB(Base):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n\nChildA() \nChildB()\n```"
