"```python\nclass Test:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.a = 10\n\n    def __call__(self): \n        b = 20\n        print(\"Inside __call__ method\")\n        print(\"Value of a:\", self.a)\n        print(\"Value of b:\", b)\n\n# Create an instance of the class\nobj = Test()\n\n# __init__ method is called when an instance is created\nprint(\"Inside __init__ method\")\nprint(\"Value of a:\", obj.a)\n\n# __call__ method is called when the object is invoked as a function\nobj()\n```\n\nExplanation: \n\n- The `__init__` method is a special method that is automatically called when an object of the class is created. It is used to initialize the attributes of the class.\n\n- The `__call__` method is a special method that allows an object to be invoked as a function. It is used to define the behavior of the object when it is called like a function.\n\n- In the given example, `__init__` method initializes the attribute `a` with the value 10. When an instance of the class is created, the `__init__` method is called automatically.\n\n- The `__call__` method is defined but not used in the given example. However, when we invoke `obj()` as a function, the `__call__` method is called. This method prints the values of `a` and `b` and a message indicating that it is inside the `__call__` method."
