"```python\nclass StaticClassExample:\n    @staticmethod\n    def get_company_name():\n        return \"CompanyName\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def get_company_address():\n        return \"CompanyAddress\"\n\n# Usage:\nprint(StaticClassExample.get_company_name())\nprint(StaticClassExample.get_company_address())\n```\n\nThis code demonstrates the basic usage of a static class in Python, which is similar to the concept of a static class in C#. The `@staticmethod` decorator is used to define a static method.\n\nYou can use a static class when you want to group related functions together, without the need to create an instance of the class. This can be useful for organizing code in a logical and maintainable way.\n\nHowever, it's generally considered better practice to avoid using static classes when possible, as they can make code harder to test and reuse. Instead, consider using regular classes with instance methods or functions that don't rely on instance state."
"```python\n# In Python, abstract classes are not directly supported like in Java or C++.\n# However, we can create a metaclass that enforces the use of abstract methods.\n\nimport abc\n\n# Define an abstract class with an abstract method\nclass MyClass(metaclass=abc.ABCMeta):\n    @abc.abstractmethod\n    def mymethod(self):\n        pass\n\n# Try to instantiate the abstract class\ntry:\n    obj = MyClass()\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(e)  # Output: Can't instantiate abstract class MyClass with abstract methods mymethod\n\n# Define a concrete class that inherits from the abstract class\nclass MyConcreteClass(MyClass):\n    def mymethod(self):\n        print(\"Concrete\")\n\n# Instantiate the concrete class and call the abstract method\nobj = MyConcreteClass()\nobj.mymethod()  # Output: Concrete\n```"
