"```python\ndef sort_multiple_columns_sql(query, columns, ascending=True):\n    # Split columns into a list\n    columns = [col.strip() for col in columns.split(',')]\n    \n    # Construct the ORDER BY clause\n    order_by_clause = \", \".join([f\"{col} {('ASC' if ascending else 'DESC')}\" for col in columns])\n    \n    # Add the ORDER BY clause to the query\n    return f\"{query} ORDER BY {order_by_clause}\"\n\n# Example usage\nquery = \"SELECT * FROM table_name\"\ncolumns = \"column1, column2\"\nsorted_query = sort_multiple_columns_sql(query, columns)\nprint(sorted_query)\n```"
"```python\ndef sort_by_index(data, index):\n    return sorted(data, key=lambda x: x[index])\n\ndata = [[1,2,3], [4,5,6], [7,8,9]]\nsorted_data = sort_by_index(data, 1)\nprint(sorted_data)\n\ndata = [(1,2,3), (4,5,6), (7,8,9)]\nsorted_data = sort_by_index(data, 1)\nprint(sorted_data)\n```\n\nThis code defines a function `sort_by_index` that takes a list of lists or tuples and an index as arguments. It uses the built-in `sorted` function with a lambda function as the key to sort the data based on the specified index. The lambda function returns the element at the specified index in each subset. The sorted data is then printed."
"# Problem Description\n\nThe given Java program is designed to parse two date strings representing times 1 second apart and compare them. However, the output of the program is not as expected, resulting in a strange difference.\n\n# Code\n\n```java\npublic static void main(String[] args) throws ParseException {\n    SimpleDateFormat sf = new SimpleDateFormat(\"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss\");\n    String str3 = \"1927-12-31 23:54:07\";\n    String str4 = \"1927-12-31 23:54:08\";\n    Date sDt3 = sf.parse(str3);\n    Date sDt4 = sf.parse(str4);\n    long ld3 = sDt3.getTime() / 1000;\n    long ld4 = sDt4.getTime() / 1000;\n    System.out.println(ld4 - ld3);\n}\n```\n\n# Explanation\n\nThe issue arises due to the timezone offset. The `SimpleDateFormat` class uses the default timezone, which is `Asia/Shanghai` in this case, with an offset of 28800000 seconds (or 8 hours) from UTC. This means that when the program calculates the time difference between `sDt3` and `sDt4`, it is actually comparing times 8 hours apart, not just 1 second.\n\nTo fix this issue, you should use the `Calendar` class to account for the timezone offset:\n\n```java\nCalendar cal = Calendar.getInstance();\ncal.setTimeZone(TimeZone.getTimeZone(\"GMT\"));\ncal.setTime(sDt3);\nlong ld3 = cal.getTimeInMillis() / 1000;\n\ncal.setTime(sDt4);\nlong ld4 = cal.getTimeInMillis() / 1000;\nSystem.out.println(ld4 - ld3);\n```\n\nAlternatively, you can use the `java.time` package, which is more accurate and easier to use:\n\n```java\nZonedDateTime zdt1 = ZonedDateTime.parse(\"1927-12-31 23:54:07\", DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(\"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss\"));\nZonedDateTime zdt2 = ZonedDateTime.parse(\"1927-12-31 23:54:08\", DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(\"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss\"));\nlong ld3 = zdt2.toInstant().toEpochMilli();\nlong ld4 = zdt1.toInstant().toEpochMilli();\nSystem.out.println(ld4 - ld3);\n```\n\nNote that the `java.time` package is available in Java 8 and later versions."
