"```python\nimport cv2\n\nvidcap = cv2.VideoCapture('Compton.mp4')\ncount = 0\nsuccess = True\n\nwhile success:\n    success, image = vidcap.read()\n    if not success:\n        break\n    cv2.imwrite(\"frame%d.jpg\" % count, image)     # save frame as JPEG file\n    if cv2.waitKey(1) == 27:                     # exit if Escape is hit\n        break\n    count += 1\n```\n\nThe original code has a few issues. The first issue is that `success, image = vidcap.read()` should be `success, image = vidcap.read()` (note the comma after `vidcap.read()`). The comma is necessary to unpack the return values of `vidcap.read()`.\n\nThe second issue is that `cv2.waitKey(10)` is too long, it should be `cv2.waitKey(1)` to check for the Escape key more frequently.\n\nThe third issue is that `success` is being reinitialized to `True` before the while loop. This is unnecessary and can cause the loop to run indefinitely. \n\nThe code now limits the frames to 1000 because `cv2.waitKey(1)` will only wait for a short period of time (1 millisecond) between each frame, allowing the program to process and save many frames quickly. If you want to limit the frames to a certain number, you can do so by adding a counter that increments every time a frame is saved, and breaking out of the loop when the counter reaches the desired number."
