"```python\nclass RESTfulProgramming:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.resources = {\n            \"users\": {\n                \"get\": \"Retrieve a list of users\",\n                \"post\": \"Create a new user\",\n                \"put\": \"Update an existing user\",\n                \"delete\": \"Delete a user\"\n            },\n            \"products\": {\n                \"get\": \"Retrieve a list of products\",\n                \"post\": \"Create a new product\",\n                \"put\": \"Update an existing product\",\n                \"delete\": \"Delete a product\"\n            }\n        }\n\n    def get_resource(self, resource):\n        return self.resources.get(resource, {}).get(\"get\", \"Resource not found\")\n\n    def create_resource(self, resource, data):\n        return self.resources.get(resource, {}).get(\"post\", \"Resource not found\")\n\n    def update_resource(self, resource, id, data):\n        return self.resources.get(resource, {}).get(\"put\", \"Resource not found\")\n\n    def delete_resource(self, resource, id):\n        return self.resources.get(resource, {}).get(\"delete\", \"Resource not found\")\n\n\n# Example usage\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    restful_programming = RESTfulProgramming()\n    print(restful_programming.get_resource(\"users\"))\n    print(restful_programming.create_resource(\"products\", {\"name\": \"New Product\", \"price\": 10.99}))\n    print(restful_programming.update_resource(\"users\", 1, {\"name\": \"John Doe\", \"email\": \"johndoe@example.com\"}))\n    print(restful_programming.delete_resource(\"products\", 1))\n```\n\nRESTful programming is an architectural style for designing networked applications. It is based on the concept of resources, which can be represented as nouns, and the idea of manipulating these resources using a fixed set of operations. The acronym REST stands for \"Representational State of Resource\" and is often used to describe this style of programming.\n\nIn a RESTful system, each resource is identified by a unique identifier, and the system provides a set of well-defined operations to interact with the resource, such as GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE. The HTTP protocol is often used to implement these operations.\n\nThe key principles of RESTful programming include:\n\n- Resource-based: Everything in RESTful programming is a resource, and resources can be identified by URIs.\n- Client-server architecture: The client and server are separate, and the client requests resources from the server.\n- Stateless: The server does not maintain any information about the client state.\n- Cacheable: Responses from the server can be cached by the client to reduce the number of requests.\n- Uniform interface: The same interface is used for all resources, and the interface is used to describe the operations that can be performed on a resource."
"# HTTP GET with request body\n\n```python\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer\nfrom urllib.parse import parse_qs, urlencode\n\nclass RequestHandler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    def do_GET(self):\n        # Parse query string\n        query_params = parse_qs(self.path[1:])\n        \n        # Parse request body\n        if self.content_length > 0:\n            body = self.rfile.read(int(self.content_length))\n            # Assuming JSON body\n            import json\n            query_params.update(json.loads(body.decode()))\n        \n        # Process query parameters\n        # Example usage:\n        # For sorting a list\n        # sorted_key = query_params.get('sort_key')\n        # sorted_direction = query_params.get('sort_direction')\n        # ...\n\n        self.send_response(200)\n        self.end_headers()\n        self.wfile.write(b'GET request with request body supported')\n\ndef run_server():\n    server_address = ('', 8000)\n    httpd = HTTPServer(server_address, RequestHandler)\n    print('Server running at http://localhost:8000/')\n    httpd.serve_forever()\n\nrun_server()\n```\n\nThe code above provides a basic example of an HTTP server that supports GET requests with request bodies. However, whether this is a good idea or not depends on the specific requirements and constraints of your application.\n\nAs for whether HTTP clients will have issues with using request bodies within a GET request, the answer is that it is not forbidden by HTTP/1.1 but it is not recommended either. The HTTP specification (RFC 2616) does not specify how to handle request bodies in GET requests, and different clients may behave differently in this regard.\n\nIn general, it is considered good practice to avoid using request bodies with GET requests, as it can make the request more verbose and may lead to issues with caching and other HTTP mechanisms."
"```python\nimport urllib.request\nimport urllib.parse\n\ndef send_post_request(url, data, headers=None):\n    # Convert data to URL encoded string\n    data = urllib.parse.urlencode(data)\n    \n    # Set headers if not provided\n    if headers is None:\n        headers = {}\n    \n    # Add Content-Type header for POST requests\n    headers['Content-Type'] = 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded'\n    \n    # Open a connection to the URL\n    with urllib.request.urlopen((url, data), headers=headers) as response:\n        # Read the response\n        return response.read()\n\n# Example usage:\nurl = 'http://example.com'\ndata = {'key': 'value'}\nheaders = {'Accept': 'text/html'}\n\nresponse = send_post_request(url, data, headers)\n\n# Print the response\nprint(response)\n```"
"```python\ndef set_no_cache_headers(response):\n    response.headers['Cache-Control'] = 'no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate'\n    response.headers['Pragma'] = 'no-cache'\n    response.headers['Expires'] = '0'\n    return response\n```\n\n```python\nfrom flask import Flask, Response\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.after_request\ndef add_header(response):\n    return set_no_cache_headers(response)\n```\n\nThis code uses Flask, a Python web framework, to set the `Cache-Control`, `Pragma`, and `Expires` headers in the HTTP response, preventing caching across all browsers listed in the requirements. The `add_header` function is used to set these headers after the request has been processed, ensuring that the headers are added to the response before it is returned to the client."
