"```python\ndef choose_encoding(data, binary=False, filename=None):\n    \"\"\"\n    Choose between application/x-www-form-urlencoded and multipart/form-data based on input data.\n\n    Args:\n        data (dict): Dictionary of form data.\n        binary (bool, optional): Whether data contains binary data. Defaults to False.\n        filename (str, optional): File name to be transferred. Defaults to None.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: The chosen content type.\n    \"\"\"\n    if binary or filename:\n        return \"multipart/form-data\"\n    elif any(not char.isascii() for char in str(data.values()).replace(\"'\", \"\")):\n        return \"multipart/form-data\"\n    elif len(data) > 1024:\n        return \"multipart/form-data\"\n    else:\n        return \"application/x-www-form-urlencoded\"\n\n\n# Example usage:\ndata = {\"key1\": \"value1\", \"key2\": \"value2\"}\nbinary_data = {\"key3\": b\"binary data\"}\n\nprint(choose_encoding(data))  # Output: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\nprint(choose_encoding(data, binary=True))  # Output: multipart/form-data\nprint(choose_encoding(data, filename=\"example.txt\"))  # Output: multipart/form-data\n```"
"# Naming Convention for Custom HTTP Headers\n\nIn HTTP, headers are case-insensitive and do not require a specific naming convention. However, following a consistent naming convention can improve readability and maintainability of your code.\n\nHere's a common convention for naming custom HTTP headers:\n\n- Use lowercase letters for the header name.\n- Use underscores (`_`) to separate words in the header name.\n- Avoid using special characters or spaces.\n- Keep the header name concise and descriptive.\n\nExample of a well-named custom HTTP header:\n```python\n'account_user_id': 123\n```\n\nThis header name clearly indicates that it contains the user ID associated with the account.\n\nSome examples of common custom HTTP headers and their uses:\n\n- `Authorization`: used to authenticate requests with tokens or credentials.\n- `Content-Type`: specifies the format of the request body.\n- `Accept-Language`: specifies the preferred language for responses.\n\nYou can add these headers to your requests or responses using the `requests` library in Python:\n```python\nimport requests\n\nheaders = {\n    'account_user_id': 123,\n    'Authorization': 'Bearer your_token'\n}\n\nresponse = requests.get('https://api.example.com/data', headers=headers)\n```"
