"```python\nfrom flask import Flask, request, jsonify\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n# In-memory object storage\nobjects = {}\n\n@app.route('/objects/<string:id>', methods=['PUT'])\ndef update_object(id):\n    object_data = request.get_json()\n    if id in objects:\n        objects[id] = object_data\n        return jsonify(object_data), 200\n    else:\n        return jsonify({'error': 'Object not found'}), 404\n\n@app.route('/objects', methods=['POST'])\ndef create_object():\n    object_data = request.get_json()\n    id = object_data['id']\n    if id in objects:\n        return jsonify({'error': 'Object already exists'}), 409\n    else:\n        objects[id] = object_data\n        return jsonify(object_data), 201\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    app.run(debug=True)\n```"
"### Idempotency of PATCH\n\n```python\nimport requests\n\ndef is_idempotent(url, method, params=None, data=None):\n    # Simulate PATCH request\n    patch_response = requests.patch(url, params=params, json=data)\n    # Simulate subsequent PATCH request\n    subsequent_patch_response = requests.patch(url, params=params, json=data)\n    \n    # Check if both requests return the same response\n    return patch_response.status_code == subsequent_patch_response.status_code\n\n# Test the function\nurl = '/users/1'\npatch_data = {'email': 'skwee357@newdomain.example'}\nprint(is_idempotent(url, 'PATCH', data=patch_data))  # Should print True\n```\n\n### Real Difference between PUT and PATCH\n\n```python\nimport requests\n\ndef put_vs_patch():\n    # PUT request to replace an entire entity\n    put_response = requests.put('/users', json={'username': 'skwee357', 'email': 'skwee357@domain.example'})\n    \n    # PATCH request to update specific fields\n    patch_response = requests.patch('/users/1', json={'email': 'skwee357@newdomain.example'})\n    \n    print(f'PUT Response: {put_response.status_code}')\n    print(f'PATCH Response: {patch_response.status_code}')\n\n# Usage\nput_vs_patch()\n```\n\nIn the provided code examples, we define a function `is_idempotent` to simulate PATCH requests and check if they return the same response. This function can be used to verify the idempotency of the PATCH method.\n\nThe `put_vs_patch` function demonstrates the usage of PUT and PATCH requests. It sends a PUT request to replace an entire entity and a PATCH request to update specific fields, highlighting the difference between these two methods."
