"```python\n# Define a dictionary\nobj = {\n    \"name\": \"Simon\",\n    \"age\": \"20\",\n    \"clothing\": {\n        \"style\": \"simple\",\n        \"hipster\": False\n    }\n}\n\n# Iterate through the object properties\nfor prop in obj:\n    # Check if the property is a key in the object (not in its nested objects)\n    if isinstance(obj[prop], dict):\n        print(f\"Property: {prop}, Value: {obj[prop]}\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"Property: {prop}, Value: {obj[prop]}\")\n```\n\nThe variable `prop` represents the keys (or properties) of the object. In the context of the provided JavaScript code, `for(var propt in obj)` iterates over the object's keys, which include the top-level keys and the keys of the nested object. However, the JavaScript code only logs the top-level keys, not the nested keys. The reason for this is due to the way the `for...in` loop works in JavaScript. It iterates over the object's own enumerable properties, including inherited properties. To avoid logging the inherited properties, the JavaScript code only checks if the property is a key in the object itself, not in its nested objects. \n\nIn the provided Python code, we use `isinstance(obj[prop], dict)` to check if the property value is a dictionary (i.e., a nested object). If it's not a dictionary, we simply print the property and its value."
