"# Differences between x:Name and Name attributes in WPF\n\nIn XAML, both `x:Name` and `Name` attributes are used to assign a unique name to an element. However, they differ in their behavior and usage.\n\n### x:Name attribute\n\nThe `x:Name` attribute is specific to XAML and is used to assign a name to an element that can be accessed programmatically using the `FindName` method in C#.\n\n```csharp\npublic partial class MainWindow : Window\n{\n    public Button button;\n\n    public MainWindow()\n    {\n        InitializeComponent();\n\n        button = (Button)FindName(\"myButton\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Name attribute\n\nThe `Name` attribute is a standard .NET attribute that can be used on any element. It can be accessed using the `ElementName` property in C#.\n\n```csharp\npublic partial class MainWindow : Window\n{\n    public MainWindow()\n    {\n        InitializeComponent();\n\n        var element = this.ElementName;\n        // Use element as needed\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Key differences\n\n- The `x:Name` attribute is specific to XAML and cannot be used directly in C# code-behind. It requires the `FindName` method to access the element.\n- The `Name` attribute is a standard .NET attribute and can be used directly in C# code-behind using the `ElementName` property.\n\n### Performance and memory implications\n\nThere are no significant performance or memory implications between using `x:Name` and `Name` attributes. However, using `x:Name` might lead to slightly slower performance due to the need to search for the element in the visual tree.\n\n### Best practice\n\n- Use `x:Name` when you need to access an element programmatically from within a XAML file.\n- Use `Name` when you need to access an element programmatically from within C# code-behind."
"from typing import Any\n\nclass Resource:\n    def __init__(self, name: str, value: Any):\n        self.name = name\n        self.value = value\n\nclass StaticResource(Resource):\n    def __init__(self, name: str, value: Any):\n        super().__init__(name, value)\n        self._loaded = False\n\n    def get_value(self) -> Any:\n        if not self._loaded:\n            self._loaded = True\n            return self.value\n        else:\n            raise Exception(\"StaticResource has already been loaded\")\n\nclass DynamicResource(Resource):\n    def __init__(self, name: str, value: Any):\n        super().__init__(name, value)\n        self._loaded = False\n\n    def get_value(self) -> Any:\n        if not self._loaded:\n            self._loaded = True\n            return self.value\n        else:\n            raise Exception(\"DynamicResource has already been loaded\")"
