"```python\nfrom tkinter import Tk, Frame, Label\n\nclass OverlayControl:\n    def __init__(self, master):\n        self.master = master\n        self.frame = Frame(master, bg='blue', highlightthickness=0)\n        self.frame.pack(fill='both', expand=True)\n        self.label = Label(self.frame, text='Overlay Control', bg='blue', fg='white')\n        self.label.pack(fill='both', expand=True)\n\nroot = Tk()\noverlay_control = OverlayControl(root)\nroot.mainloop()\n```\n\nThis code creates a Tkinter window with an overlay control that appears above all other controls. The overlay control is a blue frame with a white label. The `highlightthickness=0` parameter is used to remove the border around the frame, and `fill='both', expand=True` is used to make the frame expand to fill the available space."
"```python\n# No direct equivalent in Python, but you can achieve similar functionality using a class attribute\nclass VersionManager:\n    filter_string = \"\"\n\n# Example usage:\nversion_manager = VersionManager()\nversion_manager.filter_string = \"some_value\"\n\nprint(VersionManager.filter_string)\n```\n\nHowever, if you want to mimic the exact behavior of the C# static property, you can use a class-level variable and provide a class method to set it:\n\n```python\nclass VersionManager:\n    filter_string = \"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def set_filter_string(value):\n        VersionManager.filter_string = value\n\n# Example usage:\nVersionManager.set_filter_string(\"some_value\")\nprint(VersionManager.filter_string)\n```"
