"```python\n# Explanation: The super() function is used to access methods and properties of parent classes.\n#             It is used to call methods in parent classes when a method is defined in both the parent and child classes.\n\n# Explanation: Using super() instead of Base.__init__ allows for dynamic method calling based on the current class.\n#             It ensures that the correct parent class method is called, even if the child class has its own __init__ method.\n\nclass Base(object):\n    def __init__(self):\n        print(\"Base created\")\n\nclass ChildA(Base):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super(ChildA, self).__init__()  # Use super() with the current class\n\nclass ChildB(Base):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super(ChildB, self).__init__()  # Use super() with the current class\n\nChildA() \nChildB()\n```\n\nOutput:\n```\nBase created\nBase created\n```"
"class IoCContainer:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.services = {}\n\n    def register(self, service_name, service):\n        self.services[service_name] = service\n\n    def get_service(self, service_name):\n        return self.services.get(service_name)\n\nclass Service1:\n    def do_something(self):\n        return \"Service1\"\n\nclass Service2:\n    def do_something(self):\n        return \"Service2\"\n\ndef main():\n    ioc_container = IoCContainer()\n    service1 = Service1()\n    service2 = Service2()\n\n    ioc_container.register(\"service1\", service1)\n    ioc_container.register(\"service2\", service2)\n\n    print(ioc_container.get_service(\"service1\").do_something())\n    print(ioc_container.get_service(\"service2\").do_something())\n\nmain()"
"# Difference between self and $this in PHP\n\nIn PHP 5, both `self` and `$this` are used to refer to the current object. However, there is a difference between them.\n\n## Using `$this`\n\nIn PHP, `$this` is a global variable that refers to the current object. It is used to access the object's properties and methods.\n\n```php\nclass MyClass {\n    public $myProperty;\n\n    public function __construct($myProperty) {\n        $this->myProperty = $myProperty;\n    }\n\n    public function myMethod() {\n        echo $this->myProperty;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Using `self`\n\n`self` is a keyword that refers to the current class. It is used to access the class's properties and methods.\n\n```php\nclass MyClass {\n    public $myProperty;\n\n    public function __construct($myProperty) {\n        $this->myProperty = $myProperty;\n    }\n\n    public static function myMethod() {\n        echo self::class . ' class';\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## When to use each\n\n- Use `$this` when you need to access the current object's properties and methods.\n- Use `self` when you need to access the current class's properties and methods, but you want to distinguish it from the current object."
