"class Car:\n    def __init__(self, brand, model, year):\n        self.brand = brand\n        self.model = model\n        self.year = year\n\nclass Engine:\n    def __init__(self, horsepower):\n        self.horsepower = horsepower\n\nclass Transmission:\n    def __init__(self, gears):\n        self.gears = gears\n\nclass ElectricCar(Car):\n    def __init__(self, brand, model, year, battery_capacity):\n        super().__init__(brand, model, year)\n        self.battery_capacity = battery_capacity\n\nclass GasolineCar(Car):\n    def __init__(self, brand, model, year, engine):\n        super().__init__(brand, model, year)\n        self.engine = engine\n\nclass HybridCar(Car):\n    def __init__(self, brand, model, year, transmission, engine):\n        super().__init__(brand, model, year)\n        self.transmission = transmission\n        self.engine = engine\n\n# Composition example\nclass Truck(Car):\n    def __init__(self, brand, model, year, cargo_capacity):\n        super().__init__(brand, model, year)\n        self.cargo_capacity = cargo_capacity\n        self.engine = Engine(200)\n        self.transmission = Transmission(5)\n\nclass ElectricTruck(Truck):\n    def __init__(self, brand, model, year, battery_capacity, cargo_capacity):\n        super().__init__(brand, model, year, cargo_capacity)\n        self.battery_capacity = battery_capacity\n\n# Inheritance example\nclass EngineWithTurbo(Engine):\n    def __init__(self, horsepower, turboboost):\n        super().__init__(horsepower)\n        self.turboboost = turboboost\n\nclass CarWithTurbo(GasolineCar):\n    def __init__(self, brand, model, year, engine_with_turbo):\n        super().__init__(brand, model, year, engine_with_turbo)\n\n# Trade-offs:\n# - Inheritance allows for code reuse but can lead to tight coupling between classes.\n# - Composition allows for loose coupling and can lead to more flexible code.\n# - Inheritance is useful when there is a clear \"is-a\" relationship between classes.\n# - Composition is useful when there is a \"has-a\" relationship between classes.\n\n# When to choose inheritance:\n# - When the subclass is a modified version of the parent class.\n# - When the subclass has a clear \"is-a\" relationship with the parent class.\n# - When the subclass needs to inherit behavior from the parent class.\n\n# When to choose composition:\n# - When the parent class is a container for the child class.\n# - When the child class is not a modified version of the parent class.\n# - When the child class needs to have a flexible relationship with the parent class."
"# Difference Between @classmethod and @staticmethod in Python\n\n## What are @classmethod and @staticmethod?\n### @classmethod\nThe `@classmethod` decorator is used to define a class method. A class method is a method that belongs to a class rather than an instance of the class. It is used to create a new instance of the class or to modify the class itself.\n\n### @staticmethod\nThe `@staticmethod` decorator is used to define a static method. A static method is a method that belongs to a class but does not have access to the class or instance variables. It is used to perform some general-purpose operation that does not depend on the state of an instance.\n\n## How are they different?\n### Class Methods\n- Can access class variables\n- Can modify class variables\n- Can be used to create a new instance of the class\n- Must have a `self` parameter, but this is the class itself rather than an instance\n\n### Static Methods\n- Cannot access class variables\n- Cannot modify class variables\n- Cannot be used to create a new instance of the class\n- Do not have a `self` parameter\n\n## When to use them?\n\n- Use `@classmethod` when you want to create a new instance of the class or modify the class itself.\n- Use `@staticmethod` when you want to perform some general-purpose operation that does not depend on the state of an instance.\n\n## Example Usage\n\n```python\nclass MathOperations:\n    @classmethod\n    def add(cls, a, b):\n        return a + b\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def multiply(a, b):\n        return a * b\n\n# Using class method to create a new instance\nmath_instance = MathOperations.add(5, 10)\nprint(math_instance)  # Output: 15\n\n# Using static method\nprint(MathOperations.multiply(5, 10))  # Output: 50\n```"
"```python\ndef get_foo(self):\n    return self._foo\n\ndef set_foo(self, foo):\n    if not isinstance(foo, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"foo must be a string\")\n    self._foo = foo\n```"
"```python\n# Single and double leading underscores are used for name mangling in Python.\n# Name mangling is a mechanism to make class names or instance names harder to access directly.\n\nclass Test:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.__single_leading_underscore = \"Single leading underscore\"\n        self.__double_leading_underscore = \"Double leading underscore\"\n\nobj = Test()\nprint(obj.__single_leading_underscore)  # Output: Single leading underscore\nprint(obj.__double_leading_underscore)  # Output: Double leading underscore\n\ntry:\n    print(obj._Test__single_leading_underscore)  # Raises AttributeError\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"Double leading underscore is protected, not private.\")\n\ntry:\n    print(obj._Test__double_leading_underscore)  # Raises AttributeError\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"Double leading underscore is protected, not private.\")\n```\n\nIn the above code, we are trying to access the private variables of the class `Test`. The single leading underscore makes the variable protected, while the double leading underscore makes it private. However, Python does not provide a strict enforcement of privacy, and these variables can still be accessed using their mangled names."
"# Abstract vs Virtual Methods\n\nIn object-oriented programming, both abstract methods and virtual methods are used to achieve polymorphism. However, there's a key difference between the two.\n\n## Abstract Methods\n\nAbstract methods are declared in an abstract class, which cannot be instantiated on its own. They must be implemented by any non-abstract subclass. Abstract methods are typically used when you want to define an interface without providing any implementation.\n\n```python\nfrom abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass AbstractClassExample(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def abstract_method(self):\n        pass\n```\n\n## Virtual Methods\n\nVirtual methods are declared in a non-abstract class and can be overridden by subclasses. They are typically used when you want to provide a default implementation that can be extended by subclasses.\n\n```python\nclass VirtualClassExample:\n    def virtual_method(self):\n        return \"Default implementation\"\n```\n\n## Key Differences\n\n-   Abstract methods are part of an abstract class, while virtual methods can be part of any class.\n-   Abstract methods must be implemented by non-abstract subclasses, while virtual methods can be overridden.\n-   Abstract methods cannot be called directly on an instance of the abstract class, while virtual methods can be called directly on an instance of any class.\n\n## Recommendation\n\n-   Use abstract methods when you want to define an interface without providing any implementation, and you need to ensure that any non-abstract subclass implements this method.\n-   Use virtual methods when you want to provide a default implementation that can be extended by subclasses.\n\n## Best Approach\n\nThe best approach depends on your specific use case. If you need to define an interface without providing any implementation, abstract methods are the way to go. If you need to provide a default implementation that can be extended by subclasses, virtual methods are the better choice."
"from typing import TypeVar, Generic\n\nT = TypeVar('T')\n\nclass FootballTeam(Generic[T]):\n    def __init__(self, team_name: str, running_total: int):\n        self.team_name = team_name\n        self.running_total = running_total\n        self.players: List[T] = []\n\n    def add_player(self, player: T) -> None:\n        self.players.append(player)\n\n    def count(self) -> int:\n        return len(self.players)\n\n    def __len__(self) -> int:\n        return self.count()\n\n    def __getitem__(self, index: int) -> T:\n        return self.players[index]\n\n    def __setitem__(self, index: int, value: T) -> None:\n        self.players[index] = value\n\n    def __delitem__(self, index: int) -> None:\n        del self.players[index]\n\n    def __iter__(self) -> Iterator[T]:\n        return iter(self.players)\n\n    def __contains__(self, item: T) -> bool:\n        return item in self.players\n\n    def __str__(self) -> str:\n        return f\"FootballTeam(name='{self.team_name}', running_total={self.running_total})\"\n\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\n        return f\"FootballTeam(name='{self.team_name}', running_total={self.running_total})\""
