"```python\n# There is no direct Python equivalent to the Kubernetes API.\n# However, we can create a simple class to demonstrate how to create a pod and a deployment.\n\nclass Pod:\n    def __init__(self, name, labels):\n        self.name = name\n        self.labels = labels\n\nclass Deployment:\n    def __init__(self, name, replicas, template):\n        self.name = name\n        self.replicas = replicas\n        self.template = template\n\n# Create a pod\npod = Pod(\"my-pod\", {\"name\": \"my-pod\"})\n\n# Create a deployment\ndeployment = Deployment(\"my-deployment\", 3, {\"metadata\": {\"labels\": {\"app\": \"my-app\"}}})\n\n# The deployment doesn't have any direct references to the pod.\n# However, you could create the pod and then reference it in the deployment's template.\n# This would allow you to use the deployment's restart policy and other features.\n```\n\nNote that in the context of Kubernetes, a pod is a low-level resource that provides a logical host for one or more containers. It does not have the same features as a deployment, such as replica management or restart policies. A deployment, on the other hand, is a high-level resource that manages the lifecycle of a pod."
